WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.818
Track C, Advanced.

2
00:00:00.818 --> 00:00:07.000
We start with the question that decides everything else about running local: which model size actually fits the machine you already own.

3
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:12.013
A realistic sizing table, four-bit quantized: eight gigabytes of RAM gets you a three-to-four billion parameter model.

4
00:00:12.013 --> 00:00:13.797
Sixteen gigabytes, seven-to-eight billion.

5
00:00:13.797 --> 00:00:15.454
Thirty-two gigabytes, fourteen billion.

6
00:00:15.454 --> 00:00:17.961
Sixty-four gigabytes and up, thirty-two to seventy billion.

7
00:00:17.961 --> 00:00:20.000
Bigger RAM buys bigger models, roughly linearly.

8
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:24.081
But the size that matters more than raw parameter count is tool-calling reliability.

9
00:00:24.081 --> 00:00:29.522
A small model can write fine prose and still fail to call a tool correctly — and this app is built around tools.

10
00:00:29.522 --> 00:00:32.000
Prose quality is the wrong thing to optimize first.

11
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:38.857
Test this early, with the same prompt from Episode 9 — read the README in this folder and tell me what the project does, with the Files tool on.

12
00:00:38.857 --> 00:00:43.000
If a candidate model can't complete that reliably, its parameter count does not matter.

13
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:45.249
You are not choosing one model for everything.

14
00:00:45.249 --> 00:00:54.000
Mix local and cloud per chat: local for everyday, private work where a smaller model is plenty; cloud for the hard reasoning tasks where quality matters more than privacy or cost.

15
00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:58.026
Gotcha: there is a real quality gap between a quantized local model and a frontier cloud one.

16
00:00:58.026 --> 00:01:04.000
Set that expectation before recording anything else in this module — local is genuinely capable, not a drop-in replacement for everything.

17
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:05.619
Sizing tells you what to look for.

18
00:01:05.619 --> 00:01:10.000
Next we actually go get one — searching and downloading a GGUF straight from inside the app.

